              "id": "glog-78df6262-7668-4bba-9b87-e9cb617bcafc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar vendorPath[100];\n// ... populate vendorPath ...\nsize_t length = strlen(vendorPath);\n```\n\nConsider using:\n\n```cpp\nchar vendorPath[100];\n// ... populate vendorPath ...\nvendorPath[sizeof(vendorPath) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\nsize_t length = strlen(vendorPath);\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```cpp\nchar vendorPath[100];\n// ... populate vendorPath ...\nsize_t length = strnlen(vendorPath, sizeof(vendorPath));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library (`<cstring>` in C++, `<string.h>` in C), so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
