            {
              "id": "glog-efcc2a78-37c0-475b-873c-8eba061918d0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the provided code snippet, `malloc(bufferSize+1)`, if `bufferSize` is a user-controlled value, it can lead to potential security risks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user inputs. Never trust user inputs blindly.\n2. Use functions that limit the length of the copied data, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features and libraries that manage memory automatically, such as smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) and container classes (`std::vector`, `std::string`), which can help prevent such vulnerabilities.\n4. Regularly use static code analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities in the code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `bufferSize` is a user-controlled value, ensure to validate it before using it in `malloc`. Here is a simple fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (bufferSize > MAX_BUFFER_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(bufferSize+1);\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Continue with the rest of the code\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_BUFFER_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowed buffer size.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
