            {
              "id": "glog-5d72bf0b-2859-44d4-af29-3e2a79674560",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. The `malloc` function is used to allocate a block of memory on the heap. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The problem arises when the size of the memory block to be allocated is calculated using the `strlen` function on a string that can be manipulated by an attacker. This can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if the attacker provides a string that is longer than expected.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize input data. Never trust data provided by the user. In this case, you should ensure that the string `dirEntry->d_name` is not longer than expected before passing it to the `malloc` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability. You should define a maximum length for `dirEntry->d_name` and check the length of the string before passing it to `malloc`.\n\n```cpp\n#define MAX_NAME_LENGTH 256\n\nif (strlen(dirEntry->d_name) < MAX_NAME_LENGTH) {\n    malloc(strlen(dirEntry->d_name));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for the `malloc` function\n- `string.h` for the `strlen` function\n- `dirent.h` for the `dirEntry` structure\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that all links were active and accessible at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
