            {
              "id": "glog-cd870681-9466-4a69-8a80-86f47b1bbb22",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or can be influenced by an attacker, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `malloc(newCapacity * sizeof(*pWinAdapterBegin))`, the `newCapacity` variable could be manipulated to request a large amount of memory, potentially causing a denial of service. If `newCapacity` is a negative value, it could lead to an integer overflow, resulting in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, which could lead to buffer overflows when trying to use this memory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize input that will be used to determine the size of memory allocation.\n2. Check for integer overflows before performing memory allocation.\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `calloc`, which can help prevent integer overflows.\n4. Handle the case where `malloc` fails and returns a NULL pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (newCapacity > INT_MAX/sizeof(*pWinAdapterBegin)) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    pWinAdapterBegin = (WinAdapter*)malloc(newCapacity * sizeof(*pWinAdapterBegin));\n    if (pWinAdapterBegin == NULL) {\n        // handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the multiplication would result in an integer overflow. If it would, we handle the error. If not, we proceed with the `malloc`. After the `malloc`, we check if it returned a NULL pointer, indicating that the allocation failed, and handle the error if it did.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
