            {
              "id": "glog-8e6e2ff0-6f84-472d-a9f8-31ea076260e2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. In this case, if `queryBufferSize` is a user-controlled value, it can lead to such vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user inputs. Never trust data from users blindly.\n2. Limit the maximum size of the memory that can be allocated. This can prevent excessive memory allocation which can lead to DoS attacks.\n3. Always check the return value of `malloc()`. If the memory allocation fails, `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer. Using this pointer without checking can lead to Null Pointer Dereference vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```c++\n#define MAX_SIZE 1024\n\n// ...\n\nif (queryBufferSize > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\nvoid* buffer = malloc(queryBufferSize);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Use buffer\n\nfree(buffer);\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `queryBufferSize` is greater than a predefined maximum size. If it is, we handle the error and return. Then, we allocate the memory and check if the allocation was successful. If it wasn't, we handle the error and return. Finally, after using the buffer, we free the memory to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-4ad19de7-ae5a-40f7-836b-1201019519cd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(cszOpenCLRegKeyName)`, the vulnerability arises if `cszOpenCLRegKeyName` is not a null-terminated string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen()`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `strlen(cszOpenCLRegKeyName)` with `strnlen(cszOpenCLRegKeyName, MAX_LENGTH)`, where `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of the string.\n\n```cpp\nsize_t length = strnlen(cszOpenCLRegKeyName, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C Standard Library (`<cstring>` in C++), so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
