            {
              "id": "glog-9aa00dd1-348a-4bb6-9dd0-90ae2d39037a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(valSize)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory of `valSize` bytes and returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space. The vulnerability arises when the `malloc(valSize)` function is used improperly, leading to memory leaks, buffer overflows, or other memory corruption issues. This can lead to unpredictable program behavior, including crashes, incorrect computations, and security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc(valSize)`. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer. Failing to check for this condition can lead to null pointer dereferencing, which can cause the program to crash.\n\n2. Always initialize the allocated memory. Uninitialized memory can contain sensitive information, such as passwords, that was used by other parts of your program.\n\n3. Always free the allocated memory when you're done using it. Failing to do so can lead to memory leaks, which can cause your program to consume more and more memory over time.\n\n4. Be careful with the size of the memory you're allocating. Requesting a block of memory that's too large can cause the program to crash.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Allocate memory\nchar* buffer = (char*) malloc(valSize);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle malloc failure\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n// Initialize memory\nmemset(buffer, 0, valSize);\n\n// Use the buffer...\n\n// Free the allocated memory\nfree(buffer);\nbuffer = NULL;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc(valSize)` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
