            {
              "id": "glog-6b9a6dfd-9d6a-45e5-bfa5-f7df97cacfd4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question pertains to the improper use of the `malloc` function in C++. The `malloc` function is used to allocate a block of memory on the heap. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer. If this null pointer is not properly checked before being used, it can lead to a null pointer dereference, which can cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the result of `malloc` before using the allocated memory. If `malloc` returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation failed, and the program should handle this error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nD3DKMT_ADAPTERINFO* adapterInfo = (D3DKMT_ADAPTERINFO*) malloc(sizeof(D3DKMT_ADAPTERINFO));\nif (adapterInfo == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use adapterInfo\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we check if `malloc` returned a null pointer before using `adapterInfo`. If `malloc` failed, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: This library is required for the `malloc` function.\n- `d3dkmthk.h`: This library is required for the `D3DKMT_ADAPTERINFO` structure.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
