              "id": "glog-9ee42646-bfb4-4232-b665-5193a00df0ec",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or data corruption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string copy functions that include bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. These functions include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also lead to other issues if not used correctly, such as null-termination issues or potential off-by-one errors.\n\nAnother mitigation strategy is to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string including the null-terminating character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer alternative. For example, you can use `strncpy` like this:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(vendor->libName, libName, sizeof(vendor->libName) - 1);\nvendor->libName[sizeof(vendor->libName) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `libName` to `vendor->libName`, but it will not exceed the size of `vendor->libName`. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8204b1ea-b956-4d2a-a1d9-cd0c35abae09",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. The `malloc` function is used to allocate a block of memory on the heap. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The problem arises when the size of the memory block is calculated using the `strlen` function without considering the null character (`\\0`) at the end of the string. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, as the allocated memory block may not be large enough to hold the string and the null character.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always consider the null character when calculating the size of the memory block to be allocated. This can be done by adding 1 to the result of the `strlen` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nmalloc(strlen(libName) + 1)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdlib` (for `malloc`)\n- `cstring` (for `strlen`)\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the provided links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-4adb66b4-d14a-465f-9d35-2a3e942ace17",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, it can lead to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other types of vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n4. Use modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nVendor* vendor = nullptr;\nvendor = (Vendor*) malloc(sizeof(*vendor));\nif (vendor == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first initialize the `vendor` pointer to `nullptr`. Then, we allocate memory for `vendor` using `malloc`. After that, we check if `malloc` returned `nullptr`, which indicates that the memory allocation failed. If `malloc` failed, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-3d2aa356-f1b5-4435-8926-9558eb56939f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\nIn the given code snippet, `malloc(suffixSize)` is allocating memory of size `suffixSize`. If `suffixSize` is not properly validated or restricted, it can lead to the aforementioned vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input size before passing it to `malloc()`. Ensure that the size is within the expected range and is not excessively large.\n\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc()`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n3. Handle the case where `malloc()` fails and returns a NULL pointer. This can prevent NULL pointer dereference vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Check if suffixSize is within the expected range\nif (suffixSize > 0 && suffixSize <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(suffixSize);\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    free(buffer);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` is the maximum size that you expect `suffixSize` to be. Adjust this value according to your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2b5c5d34-b683-414d-a7df-20db6f50fa19",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check for the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source is larger than the destination. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming, and can lead to serious security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use functions that limit the amount of data copied to the size of the destination buffer. Functions such as `strncpy`, `strncat`, `snprintf`, and `vsnprintf` are safer alternatives to `memcpy`. Additionally, always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied data, including the null-terminating character for strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memcpy` function with a safer alternative. For example, you can use `std::copy` from the `<algorithm>` library, which checks the size of the destination before copying:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::copy(&api_hash, &api_hash + sizeof(api_hash), marker.apiPsoHash);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `memcpy`\n- `<algorithm>` for `std::copy`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-d2fc43df-4ff4-40f5-bed1-ea8fa8cd4ba0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is determined by `platformCount * sizeof(cl_platform_id)`. If `platformCount` is a large value, it can lead to an integer overflow, resulting in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected. This can lead to buffer overflow when trying to use this memory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-controlled or external inputs to determine the size of the memory to be allocated. If it's necessary to use such inputs, make sure to implement proper error checking and handling mechanisms.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (platformCount > INT_MAX / sizeof(cl_platform_id)) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    malloc(platformCount * sizeof(cl_platform_id));\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if `platformCount` is too large that it can cause an integer overflow. If it is, we handle the error appropriately. Otherwise, we proceed with the memory allocation.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using OpenCL, so the following libraries are required:\n\n- OpenCL\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-93c3e676-ad40-420a-9022-bf6abf840305",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to determine the length of a null-terminated string. However, if the input to `strlen` is not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows or segmentation faults. This is because `strlen` does not check the bounds of the input string, and if the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially accessing memory out of bounds.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate input strings to ensure they are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer string handling functions like `strnlen` which allows specifying a maximum length to prevent reading out of bounds.\n3. **Memory Management**: Ensure that all strings are properly allocated and null-terminated.\n4. **Static Analysis**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Original vulnerable code\n// size_t length = strlen(name);\n\n// Fixed code using strnlen\nsize_t safe_strlen(const char* name, size_t max_len) {\n    return strnlen(name, max_len);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const char* name = \"example\";\n    size_t max_len = 100; // Define a reasonable maximum length\n    size_t length = safe_strlen(name, max_len);\n    std::cout << \"Length of the string is: \" << length << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output stream operations.\n- `<cstring>`: For C-style string handling functions.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            }
